# Line-ending policy, committed rather than left to each developer's
# `core.autocrlf`. Without this file the behaviour lives only in local git
# config — this machine has core.autocrlf=true, which is why every `git add`
# printed "LF will be replaced by CRLF". That works here and silently
# differs on a collaborator's machine, and inconsistent normalization is one
# of the two classic causes of "why does my editor show thousands of
# changes" (the other being ignored files being counted).
#
# text=auto: git decides per-file, stores LF in the repo, checks out the
# platform-native ending. Deterministic for everyone, no churn.
* text=auto

# Always LF regardless of platform: these are read by tools that care.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Never touched — declared explicitly so a stray CRLF conversion can never
# corrupt one.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.db binary
*.m4a binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.webm binary

# Generated, and large: collapse it in diffs and PR review rather than
# scrolling past 1583 lines of derived types.
apps/web/src/api/schema.d.ts linguist-generated=true
apps/web/package-lock.json linguist-generated=true
docs/api/openapi.json linguist-generated=true
